#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234976 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235285 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58a49d00-cc58-5228-af38-9369cf31d8c8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor When Direction Reverses"}] :question/id #uuid "aeaa36a2-41b5-5b7b-9797-f39df1e34050" :question/math-academy-id "e-26144" :question/problem "Consider the picture below. \n\n![](images/d4/d4f18c3567d0c55d3d6e002fbe2c6ac50b97b7adc14b016c8e95006de98ca15d.png)\n\nGiven that ![](images/a7/a77e5fef143d51d984ce41363465af5f210291f6355b59da4302a944b6a49197.png) find ![](images/8a/8aef212f0b07b6c0fb04e3b3c0e415f3c93d698d48414bc0860a6405db20099b.png)" :question/worked-solution "To find ![](images/ae/ae4e47577b23025037443e79415dc50ff81b984cac5c4bb82421f985cfe4d69b.png) we want to match the expression\n![](images/bd/bd1ef582b76affe848465f42a9c78e897d93f1a2b535b7e880da8ab016561451.png)\n\nWe are given that one of the components is ![](images/dc/dc10081b11a8cfba96ed4d16d9ca1c6448af7f6198a5550955c2d123a657d339.png) So, we first draw the vector ![](images/7e/7efe1d8a24967d9509e73d84e1fb73eea1df1c2f113360cd3a34d8ef29df62e9.png) from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point ![](images/06/06046901b5ecb50bfedb17ffaf4c19cf690e50bd2e6e7c668fa59787ce4d71b7.png)\n\n![](images/65/65a6b5743ab9b6ef820744d0d6601b7dfd8de08a3f895661d96d2fe2cab8e0a7.png)\n\nLet ![](images/49/492123d15daf8306f0f5f893c7230d60f9eb8312d17b20f9a331f40f36b69b48.png) be the endpoint of the vector ![](images/d8/d8c5a62b30eeb1bc2e35d95dfc5d964dd00e512f3f7b1b53379755b1ebff5129.png) Then\n\n![](images/2d/2d7c2df783579edf0ca151150eeb55471b3f3fc2afc4e7836678b25670b484c4.png)\n\nIn ![](images/3e/3e50bb6908d0311cbf9fe09b7a8039c9d438eeda7d312f7fa87d6aa835523d49.png) we have:\n\n- ![](images/9a/9a61fb0fb1ada64abe8bd22d8b6f14a8248212de90bff583ebd1881bdf21efc5.png)\n\n- ![](images/af/af03af84c1a16b4d534cc63ac9a1b3d8d5b7d181b32cb23190deb192d4d8b8b1.png) and ![](images/d9/d9dc9bcbe34586fe4e368536ed74377c765274e2e5b008e1fee5d65e3b5008c0.png) have opposite directions\n\nHence,\n![](images/da/da5024a9515dce187468fd87b63acfd0fd89bfd867a9f03388782221604a3e74.png)\n\nSimilarly,\n\n- ![](images/cb/cb4b9bd2f50fd4b2da78369b90c522bc2b7ee20cbafa9460b643889733487c5b.png)\n\n- ![](images/5f/5fdd95564252a9a4218055ca7670a535233aa026955b7e410700e26c4f7d170c.png) and ![](images/1f/1f42d4df263db3845ca2361b8f89c4075e0cdb9b39e5564c39ae82115932a201.png) have opposite directions\n\nHence,\n![](images/da/da69cf085ea0816397356abf0878c798ab653c04fe34a51b8b849610d57778d4.png)\n\nAdditionally,\n\n![](images/57/5765a85c90552570f9a2a041f2f324a9fa0ce183d42708344531a13fbee802c8.png)\n\nConsequently,\n![](images/57/578802d62c9ab4cfbc8f250975b5b7be1396907593ba95901b8b21ad6a116d04.png)\n\nSince ![](images/46/466863f8c7522ac89896a4233a823be02940535097c28328fa2c9e9e1f4bc3d8.png) the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of ![](images/37/3720c5887713d3f7a29be384fe5e883390935b2b9b469af626a8dba968f4da8b.png) we get\n\n![](images/1c/1c2480355dc4e29a136a3bd7b6855e076f1c9698507523cafbd934521b91c6bf.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096451 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096652 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "45b6a076-8795-5510-aabc-62a925826dd0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Scalar Factors in a Linear Combination"}] :question/id #uuid "dccb5bac-e528-5b42-9949-36b3870fdde1" :question/math-academy-id "e-4287" :question/problem "Consider the picture below. Given that $\\mathbf{m}=\\lambda\\mathbf{n}+\\mu\\mathbf{k}$, find $\\lambda$ and $\\mu$.\n\n![](images/b8/b8abf22d14f93a38c4d01f368b8171e4ccfe90086d932f5b58453baa46391405.png)" :question/worked-solution "![](images/76/76b9867a7c1ef484922241ebe44272f0104e406c31310bf36227a13850f50d8b.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup ONM$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $ON=2|\\mathbf{n}|$;\n\n- $\\overrightarrow{ON}$ and $\\mathbf{n}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overrightarrow{ON}=2\\mathbf{n}$.\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $NM=2|\\mathbf{k}|$\n\n- $\\overrightarrow{NM}$ and $\\mathbf{k}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overrightarrow{NM}=2\\mathbf{k}$.\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\overset{\\to }{ON}+\\overset{\\to }{NM} \\\\  & =2n+2k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{m}=\\lambda\\mathbf{n}+\\mu\\mathbf{k}=2\\mathbf{n}+2\\mathbf{k}$. \n\nSince $\\mathbf{n} \\nparallel \\mathbf{k},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{n}$ and $\\mathbf{k}$, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda=2$ and $\\mu=2$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096452 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096653 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "feae36e7-ae88-5f3a-957f-e65a92e399ad" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Vector That Satisfies a Ratio in Terms of Known Vectors"}] :question/id #uuid "09d593cc-526e-5691-9a18-efff515eae59" :question/math-academy-id "e-4288" :question/problem "Consider the following diagram, where $\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf{a},$ $\\overrightarrow{OB}=\\mathbf{b},$ and $X$ divides $AB$ in the ratio $3:1.$ Find $\\overrightarrow{OX}.$\n\n![](images/67/678ea2936b8a5b1ded74cb8a0875c629e8be0f7002c98492ebf2e8109fc603ef.png)" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{OX} = \\overrightarrow{OA}+\\overrightarrow{AX}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overrightarrow{OA} = \\mathbf{a},$ so we just need to work out $\\overrightarrow{AX}.$\n\nFirst, from $\\triangle OAB,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{AO}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-a+b \\\\  & =b-a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $AX:XB=3:1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{AX}=\\dfrac{3}{4}\\overrightarrow{AB}=\\dfrac{3}{4}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OX} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AX} \\\\  & =a+\\frac{3}{4}(b-a) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}a+\\frac{3}{4}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096495 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096701 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fbb8f19-f35a-59c4-9b77-8f6c3542ef28" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor in a Linear Combination"}] :question/id #uuid "839ed5f5-165f-5de5-aba1-551ac605475f" :question/math-academy-id "e-5212" :question/problem "Consider the picture below.\n\n![](images/f2/f2518c2e998e7c7f551548a4cc7283720a66428ea8fa9872494a81cc3848ba9e.png)\n\nGiven that ![](images/c6/c6714d0a6eaa5c7d354d46e8d6d60da7f3d2011df0fc75773036cf7ae510c5e3.png), find ![](images/24/24878591902884f45f14f5c447bc326586a411a8870b205fc12825a4ab2c738a.png)" :question/worked-solution "Recall that when two known vectors are not parallel, a linear combination of them is unique. So, once we rewrite ![](images/5c/5c644b15efcf5a4274de23242a722604ead2ff800ea29ba7a650729227fefdf0.png) in terms of ![](images/27/27f36b1d1846e12e3c50b7d4e47f5745727792d52a48e4d85b689ec36e3d25a0.png) and ![](images/a7/a7b9033c421072576aaf959980d778c319a8ed0f849e08db644b63bc6994088c.png) we can match coefficients to determine the unknown scalar.\n\nTo find ![](images/16/166c90a9c29f99347bff6b397f31d705ec6407a19b4d71246f98308bbd7a5473.png) we want to match the expression\n![](images/fc/fc79367916dd7671c17de4f40b3d83f91d41c2f9988645964de0b53e5b11ab9b.png)\n\nWe are given that one of the components is ![](images/91/91f686c6ca02e85c386cb0f9e80b66beface446d3a541952e0ac91738ad0db37.png) So, we first draw the vector ![](images/d5/d54b9a8919c7b6106fcf5770c62b1823605d7be98a55d5333e73bcfb7528997a.png) from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point ![](images/6e/6e1bbbe9eb66856824ff70d16cc2c077519d361546b226a67146e20f15a24dd0.png)\n\n![](images/ea/ea517ac4ca8e0bcf7ba48f23e66f100323d5485f1221219aacfbea9148f4a1af.png)\n\nNote that in ![](images/f1/f1c944e6afa4e9cd84bc68004a2bfd918d81498a727aeb7c56ec627806bf93f6.png), we have:\n\n- ![](images/b7/b7ac390c4e43b2fa2fbb4063632f98869256cea877caa5e5dad4aad33ae94722.png)\n\n- ![](images/7e/7e5f2b01647ab01052fff18ab760273eebe19d47b56051b1953adf587d7f9fb3.png) and ![](images/78/784225d11bf94b7fbd67f8e2395695141eb75b1cb44f89d49b29bb3a2031534b.png) have the same direction\n\nHence, ![](images/21/21261c8ad0d86a9e7300ffc58c57fd76beef9f627492bcfe2051d5e3cfd82a4e.png)\n\nAdditionally,\n![](images/ae/ae9c98ded3aa4317a729571710ad38d973a2d67b5faeb9ad26af7c24ee5c3193.png)\n\nConsequently, ![](images/ae/aee3287ad64a654880216142756811bd4d5232b3dba2f48378b8b1c5241e4316.png)\n\nSince ![](images/46/466863f8c7522ac89896a4233a823be02940535097c28328fa2c9e9e1f4bc3d8.png) the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of ![](images/37/3720c5887713d3f7a29be384fe5e883390935b2b9b469af626a8dba968f4da8b.png) we get:\n![](images/4a/4ac145b710c5354b44a4ea1dc820d70f54b5c17985f078fce6313b671ec26fd9.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572666 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096701 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fbb8f19-f35a-59c4-9b77-8f6c3542ef28" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor in a Linear Combination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0730b596-e8ba-5761-8d72-995459b9f482" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572322} {:answer/id #uuid "326a02a6-28fe-5680-bcbd-26768b11a79b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572374} {:answer/id #uuid "5de4b890-ee0f-528b-8086-4f0f53ba2eda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572429} {:answer/id #uuid "65dbfb77-dae6-5079-bb06-2fd6cdbe211b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572436} {:answer/id #uuid "89ca97dd-07f7-5d11-a1c6-2ec6ea64ebb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572463}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89ca97dd-07f7-5d11-a1c6-2ec6ea64ebb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572463} :answer-field/id #uuid "d13d9e16-1b93-5d32-9389-dc37d592577d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572647}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "049a751e-712d-54cc-ad6d-c6e3564444d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-120247" :question/problem "![](images/2e/2ea6acbf6574e970a798100729de9a92874bca90b8153db9883daa1cd6d98c2a.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a+2b$, find $\\lambda .$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+2b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $2b.$ So, we first draw the vector $2b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/3c/3c31e9b0d926de6a3f8c9f35bd816349d6488c8b98864d8576fdbfc395bbd92d.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $2b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=3|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=3a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =2b+3a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+2b=3a+2b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =3\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364880 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096701 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fbb8f19-f35a-59c4-9b77-8f6c3542ef28" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor in a Linear Combination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5e92f9d-c8dc-5823-afa8-eae130a1ba43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364882} {:answer/id #uuid "3de53034-0a04-5d26-b49f-128745f4ea0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364883} {:answer/id #uuid "f76caff6-871d-509a-b53c-70f664edb2f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364884} {:answer/id #uuid "8993d132-186e-5f70-b539-0ecadbfbfd21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364885} {:answer/id #uuid "7bd3c80c-11f7-505d-a02f-11ce6326b3f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364886}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7bd3c80c-11f7-505d-a02f-11ce6326b3f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364886} :answer-field/id #uuid "af541ef0-9d16-554c-878d-707d965733bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364881}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "49639a9a-7a73-58c8-8e2e-c3ff76e8e2ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-120250" :question/problem "![](images/e1/e1391a86176b1453c05f6ab43f2b5360fac00287e621d8917a142116990688b2.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a+3b$, find $\\lambda .$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+3b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $3b.$ So, we first draw the vector $3b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/ea/eadc3cc79d5c0f9b58bd8afc6864dca8e5f794cf398921e2ad540b491819562f.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $3b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =3b+a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+3b=a+3b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =1\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572725 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096652 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "45b6a076-8795-5510-aabc-62a925826dd0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Scalar Factors in a Linear Combination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e8cd8a3-5848-5337-929a-567d0fe4c28c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =3$ and $\\mu =2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572355} {:answer/id #uuid "58536380-845e-5010-b39b-f5e39e4c551c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572422} {:answer/id #uuid "a9a75f3a-4920-5e33-be0c-78e376d995c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572497} {:answer/id #uuid "bb1a8c24-4115-5d89-a601-bd095e5bfa7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-2$ and $\\mu =-3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572523} {:answer/id #uuid "e054b3a2-0eea-5210-8737-47555b3de52a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-2$ and $\\mu =2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572562}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9a75f3a-4920-5e33-be0c-78e376d995c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572497} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e94447c-6428-5f0d-9958-0d3458e2b5ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572623}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eeb1828d-7e99-5d6a-aa95-0e286af84c7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-120265" :question/problem "![](images/67/6715bda9432c2ba3e61c31f436cd6b7608312da499bb4372944d25db6cf76090.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a+\\mu b$, find $\\lambda$ and $\\mu .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/89/8997fda665e4bba248dd19f5b8fe0d2a2524df341042271a0f8426e0ce152ecd.png)\n\nIn $△OAC$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OA=3|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OA}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=-3a.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $AC=2|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $b$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=-2b.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =-3a-2b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+\\mu b=-3a-2b.$ \n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =-2$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572708 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096652 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "45b6a076-8795-5510-aabc-62a925826dd0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Scalar Factors in a Linear Combination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0830e01b-93be-55a3-ac87-38d68196c9be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\mu =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572323} {:answer/id #uuid "1bd72a4f-8a2a-5d84-a5df-17bc127c8a72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-2$ and $\\mu =\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572348} {:answer/id #uuid "8ed3d896-e6f3-5e0d-9156-7213b2534219" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\mu =\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572467} {:answer/id #uuid "a2644a20-0b59-59e2-bf9a-fb12db4f0be3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-2$ and $\\mu =-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572490} {:answer/id #uuid "a306cce2-e35e-56e8-a262-e2dc8808954f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572492}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0830e01b-93be-55a3-ac87-38d68196c9be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\mu =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572323} :answer-field/id #uuid "3810e182-6568-500c-b95b-e07cc9fea34d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572610}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ada3d5e7-07f2-5981-9fcd-59fe51195895" :question/math-academy-id "q-120273" :question/problem "![](images/5d/5dea155246de4f35697780db7ebf1802cdf5c2899fe42fa494993be2f65dd7f3.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $h=\\lambda f+\\mu g$, find $\\lambda$ and $\\mu .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/99/9905448ad59cb2f68f5def832811eb34056305d1a535f23387f4403a340cb8f8.png)\n\nIn $△OFH$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OF=\\frac{1}{2}|f|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OF}$ has the opposite direction to $f$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OF}=-\\frac{1}{2}f.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $FH=2|g|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{FH}$ has the opposite direction to $g$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{FH}=-2g.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h & =\\overset{\\to }{OF}+\\overset{\\to }{FH} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}f-2g.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $h=\\lambda f+\\mu g=-\\frac{1}{2}f-2g.$\n\nSince $f∦g,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\mu =-2$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097046 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096653 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "feae36e7-ae88-5f3a-957f-e65a92e399ad" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Vector That Satisfies a Ratio in Terms of Known Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95092ddd-a592-51a9-a935-ee8d21d09f58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "OT^(→) = a + \\frac{1}{2}b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094250} {:answer/id #uuid "c8ea1899-3edc-5a44-a68d-8e0dc35e8f3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "OT^(→) = \\frac{2}{3}a + \\frac{1}{3}b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094251} {:answer/id #uuid "0f332864-f317-5e8c-afc1-79039bba7d4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "OT^(→) = \\frac{1}{3}a + \\frac{2}{3}b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094252} {:answer/id #uuid "149f3ec8-5bfe-5cff-b544-5450e508dd7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "OT^(→) = \\frac{1}{2}a + b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094253} {:answer/id #uuid "28977d08-9521-501a-8c2b-abbc30e9104f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "OT^(→) = \\frac{1}{2}a + \\frac{1}{2}b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094254}] :answer-field/id #uuid "7ba0bf42-9d3f-5ea8-846a-8da902ababbd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094249} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4bff8291-52e2-5378-8c8d-7d44f23a4c69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\frac{1}{2}a+b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364895} {:answer/id #uuid "34011083-f78c-5655-a0da-dc9cb40aca66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\frac{1}{2}a+\\frac{1}{2}b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364896} {:answer/id #uuid "485389db-c4a8-5142-9541-d78c1ba0024d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\frac{2}{3}a+\\frac{1}{3}b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364897} {:answer/id #uuid "3a64dc1f-5834-51d0-838e-b496cd2b0fdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\frac{1}{3}a+\\frac{2}{3}b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364898} {:answer/id #uuid "15eb2c14-0598-5915-a3c6-298fc96e1bc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OT}=a+\\frac{1}{2}b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364899}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34011083-f78c-5655-a0da-dc9cb40aca66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\frac{1}{2}a+\\frac{1}{2}b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364896} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ab7ddb7-0e0f-504f-b6a0-52fbf88f18aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364894}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a844b5df-969a-5ad4-9ad2-fd01250ec5ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-12212" :question/problem "![](images/c2/c2406e6f3cb76f50bfcc73a348b38d93b7a24da8e4314e0b3523bb4cbf143364.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=a,$ $\\overset{\\to }{OB}=b$, and $T$ divides $\\overset{―}{AB}$ in the ratio $AT:TB=1:1.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{OT}.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AT}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=a,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{AT}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OAB,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{AO}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-a+b \\\\  & =b-a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $AT:TB=1:1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{AT}=\\frac{1}{2}\\overset{\\to }{AB}=\\frac{1}{2}(b-a).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OT} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AT} \\\\  & =a+\\frac{1}{2}(b-a) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}a+\\frac{1}{2}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256624 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096653 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "feae36e7-ae88-5f3a-957f-e65a92e399ad" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Vector That Satisfies a Ratio in Terms of Known Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36986ba5-c1c7-5ea3-9029-fe6fbc5d146c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OC}=\\frac{1}{3}a-\\frac{2}{3}b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254139} {:answer/id #uuid "3bd530a5-d4e3-580e-889c-5ada349d4218" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OC}=\\frac{5}{6}a+\\frac{1}{6}b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254191} {:answer/id #uuid "4a0a0246-d60a-56af-9f8c-69a95f4900db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OC}=\\frac{1}{6}a+\\frac{5}{6}b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254307} {:answer/id #uuid "8fa154b7-9e05-5e8f-bab5-281720591409" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OC}=\\frac{1}{3}a+\\frac{2}{3}b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254929} {:answer/id #uuid "fa0d825b-907e-52e1-abed-caa127c11e30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OC}=\\frac{1}{6}a-\\frac{5}{6}b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255861}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a0a0246-d60a-56af-9f8c-69a95f4900db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OC}=\\frac{1}{6}a+\\frac{5}{6}b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254307} :answer-field/id #uuid "7bbfddf2-46ce-5bb3-a95d-a3137cfc6517" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256150}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "61f6b767-fa3e-521f-814f-8371f7adf99c" :question/math-academy-id "q-12235" :question/problem "![](images/45/45110269c8a1ca9ad80cabc2f6c44592dca781afde7347d9d0a7e3363a76563a.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=a,$ $\\overset{\\to }{OB}=b$ and $C$ divides $\\overset{―}{AB}$ in the ratio $AC:CB=5:1.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{OC}.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OC}=\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=a,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{AC}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OAB,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{AO}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-a+b \\\\  & =b-a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $AC:CB=5:1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{AC}=\\frac{5}{6}\\overset{\\to }{AB}=\\frac{5}{6}(b-a).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OC} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =a+\\frac{5}{6}(b-a) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}a+\\frac{5}{6}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572695 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096701 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fbb8f19-f35a-59c4-9b77-8f6c3542ef28" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor in a Linear Combination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57534557-824c-57ce-9724-420aeeed76a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572420}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57534557-824c-57ce-9724-420aeeed76a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572420} :answer-field/id #uuid "f419c0b9-3927-593f-9535-801465b684b3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572659}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "63f90954-aef6-5358-9e26-c4da4dbb21fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-327052" :question/problem "![](images/22/22f309ffc9ff6ae4a43a5faae12e4ed952d65aec70f87a7e71f64594f0c61f92.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $c=\\frac{4}{3}a+\\lambda b,$ then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\frac{4}{3}a+\\lambda b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $\\frac{4}{3}a.$ So, we first draw the vector $\\frac{4}{3}a$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/b8/b8db5015e22e55282e180b1d81f7a2e1ca544dfe0f468dc4dedb6283bac2da5c.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $\\frac{4}{3}a.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=\\frac{1}{4}|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $b$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=\\frac{1}{4}b.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}a+\\frac{1}{4}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\frac{4}{3}a+\\lambda b=\\frac{4}{3}a+\\frac{1}{4}b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $b,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235567 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235285 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58a49d00-cc58-5228-af38-9369cf31d8c8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor When Direction Reverses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d6d4db2-2d84-5cf9-81e1-041f57f2cacc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234640} {:answer/id #uuid "4a392df7-ad5d-5be9-b18a-e4f744d5c092" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234660} {:answer/id #uuid "8ed9ea91-ee70-5a16-94a9-6b29294fcbcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234766} {:answer/id #uuid "e9b13819-3205-5667-bc39-1375026621c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234916} {:answer/id #uuid "fb1af85e-3e3c-595d-9837-0ef51ee523a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234942}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb1af85e-3e3c-595d-9837-0ef51ee523a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234942} :answer-field/id #uuid "e877b6bf-02c3-53e5-9ea3-6d64098261ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235250}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5b87f1b1-b01d-5f2b-86fb-046c3cada5b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-367051" :question/problem "![](images/0f/0f49938e84521acbfe0e44190190ae8b4fe58687a3cc241dbe9fc4a3480e37e8.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a+4b,$ find $\\lambda .$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+4b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $4b.$ So, we first draw the vector $4b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/6a/6af6aff8a4aa111e6eb74f8046a4ec01582c1cee7fcf922e443d17d0348da7f4.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $4b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=3|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=-3a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =4b-3a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+4b=-3a+4b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-3\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338753 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096701 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fbb8f19-f35a-59c4-9b77-8f6c3542ef28" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor in a Linear Combination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4416194-05a6-5e46-bc9c-4c7b0b05b834" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337775} {:answer/id #uuid "d97d4b30-65f6-5e94-8ced-509f035a553f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337924} {:answer/id #uuid "ea12c352-cf2e-574d-8e8b-85bb3bd14e46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338074} {:answer/id #uuid "eecb979e-b097-506c-b7e4-d9bb4c85501e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338120} {:answer/id #uuid "ef332865-a8b3-594f-849a-8f281bcf7db5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338124}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea12c352-cf2e-574d-8e8b-85bb3bd14e46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338074} :answer-field/id #uuid "3eeee45a-663f-54d1-ac18-7c5bae3b9518" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338388}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1d2b32e7-2ad4-5f8a-a95d-8e55e6774201" :question/math-academy-id "q-70140" :question/problem "![](images/75/7578bb2e5b594161744d96d918fa75e0225e723138d010dba3ed635098e75ca2.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=a+\\lambda b,$ find $\\lambda .$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=a+\\lambda b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $a.$ So, we begin with the vector $a,$ and then find the remaining vector needed to reach the point $C.$\n\n![](images/47/4797ffa54b3c2e4d177a6c3d527b1aaf3f1460d3c509bca86395e5f360314d25.png)\n\nNote that in $△OAC,$ we have:\n\n- $AC=3|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $b$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=3b.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =a+3b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=a+\\lambda b=a+3b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $b,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =3\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572704 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096701 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fbb8f19-f35a-59c4-9b77-8f6c3542ef28" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor in a Linear Combination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8531da3a-3a08-5b28-869a-8b5faccae9ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572460} {:answer/id #uuid "b081c18c-b180-5eb0-8c11-e10c3e68cddf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572510} {:answer/id #uuid "d8d77447-c1f0-57ce-85f8-0d94c6932498" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572556} {:answer/id #uuid "e6653684-3eb7-543a-b870-e6c60c1544db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572567} {:answer/id #uuid "faf87587-6680-5ce8-9dce-68c1abd43d20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572586}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d8d77447-c1f0-57ce-85f8-0d94c6932498" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572556} :answer-field/id #uuid "03796815-dbdc-551f-b90d-8813f015cb09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572589}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "95085e88-c005-5feb-bf13-36ae5ea5d897" :question/math-academy-id "q-70144" :question/problem "![](images/69/695c825905b268e40b7c77c681c608fcedfed4d0c5150f76224c4e60eb8ab045.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a+2b$, find $\\lambda .$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+2b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $2b.$ So, we first draw the vector $2b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/7d/7d388cdd1db5cfe59b447c3fc27b597fc4e42397966935592f56c1c9f4a598e6.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $2b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =2b+a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+2b=a+2b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =1\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235581 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235285 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58a49d00-cc58-5228-af38-9369cf31d8c8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor When Direction Reverses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "202648e4-1ed8-51ca-8741-ad33c02bbbbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234597} {:answer/id #uuid "28fcad39-0558-51c0-81b5-8737afc77511" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234609} {:answer/id #uuid "c93d8a53-26a7-5744-b10b-5b8f0da3c405" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234861} {:answer/id #uuid "da616d4e-0a1e-55df-a589-db736030d4b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234889} {:answer/id #uuid "e79cd088-e254-50a4-9040-d34103eae50a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234910}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c93d8a53-26a7-5744-b10b-5b8f0da3c405" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234861} :answer-field/id #uuid "9706ed72-1b58-5bbe-8976-330e0740d99e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235155}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a9d1dc6a-3cc4-5a49-870f-4f180b06cd63" :question/math-academy-id "q-70148" :question/problem "![](images/d3/d3f77b108decdfe95fe06d1ace87ee7f844a4f937f8b67a9f14e750a09ae4d57.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a-b,$ find $\\lambda .$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a-b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $-b.$ So, we first draw the vector $-b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/7f/7f29124776f69f370b3129313fd1c2fa850924f446c6f086dcacd170f1ca6d24.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $-b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=2.5|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=-2.5a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =-b-2.5a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a-b=-2.5a-b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-2.5\n$$"}]}
